WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.400
Good to have you here. It's July 14th, 2026. Every story we cover starts with someone who had a

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:15.560
reasonable plan. In 2016, a Seattle software engineer named Marcus kept a 347-day duo

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:23.380
lingo streak alive by completing a single two-minute lesson at 1147 p meters, while standing in his

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:30.200
kitchen after a 14-hour workday. He had begun the app two years earlier to learn Spanish for an

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:36.760
upcoming trip. By the time the trip was canceled, he was no longer tracking vocabulary, but simply

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:42.800
protecting the number on the screen. The same pattern appeared in offices that adopted productivity

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:48.780
platforms, promising fun point systems, and in running clubs where Strava segments turned neighborhood

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:55.180
routes into daily competitions measured to the meter. Game designers and behavioral researchers

00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:01.760
had spent the 2000s mapping what kept players returning to screens. Immediate feedback, visible

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:09.440
progress, social comparison, and variable rewards all played a part. Around 2010, several practitioners

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:16.200
including Gabe Ziterman and Rajat Paharia of Bunchball argued that these same loops could solve

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:21.460
persistent engagement problems in education, corporate wellness, and personal fitness.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:29.400
At the time, smartphone adoption was rising quickly. Attention data showed sharp drop-offs in self-directed

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:36.200
study and exercise routines. Laboratory work on extrinsic rewards appeared promising for short-term

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:42.660
behavior change. The designers viewed low motivation not as a character flaw, but as a design problem

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:49.020
that clearer goals and instant feedback could correct. Their proposals therefore emphasized

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:53.240
voluntary adoption and measurable activity rather than coercion.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:02:00.680
Duolingo launched publicly in 2012 with experience points, levels, and daily street counters.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:08.520
Strava had already introduced segment leaderboards in 2009 and grew rapidly after its mobile app in

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:16.380
2011. Snapchat added streaks in 2015 displaying consecutive messaging days between users.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:23.000
Corporate platforms such as Bunchball and later Microsoft workplace gamification experiments

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.540
followed similar patterns. Early metrics looked favorable. Duolingo reported millions of daily

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:36.080
active users within two years. Strava documented large increases in logged rides and runs.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:42.620
Proponents pointed to these numbers as proof that game elements converted sporadic intentions into

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:49.720
repeated action. A minority of psychologists drawing on Edward Desi and Richard Ryan's self-determination

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:57.560
theory from the 1970s and 1980s cautioned that external rewards could eventually reduce internal drive.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:02.580
Yet those warnings remained largely outside product roadmaps at the time.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:08.260
Users began optimizing for the visible score rather than the underlying activity.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:14.660
Duolingo learners selected lessons that awarded the most experience points instead of those that

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:21.480
addressed actual weaknesses. Some completed the same beginner exercise repeatedly to maintain streaks.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:28.260
Strava riders chose routes that maximized segment points sometimes at the expense of safety or

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:33.980
enjoyment. A subset reported training through injuries to avoid losing weekly totals.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:40.840
Workplace point systems produced similar distortions. Employees prioritized tasks that generated

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:47.760
visible badges over higher value work that did not. Longitudinal studies later indicated that once

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:54.280
the novelty of points faded, many participants experienced elevated stress tied to the fear of losing

00:03:54.280 --> 00:04:01.100
accumulated progress. The causal mechanism was straightforward. The systems measured and rewarded

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:07.920
frequency and visibility so behavior migrated toward what was measured. Second order effects included

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:14.160
reduced willingness to attempt difficult material that risked temporary score drops and a growing

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:21.600
sense that rest or unstructured practice was wasteful. By the late 2000s teens, mental health surveys

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:27.820
among heavy users of streak based apps began showing correlations with anxiety and sleep disruption,

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:33.980
particularly among adolescents whose social standing became tied to visible snapchat numbers.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:41.780
Several platforms introduced modifications once user fatigue became evident. Duolingo added streak

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:48.800
freezes and later practice modes that deemphasized scoring. Strava offered optional private routes and

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:55.680
muted segment notifications for some users. A few school districts scaled back class dojo style point

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:02.560
systems after parent complaints about public comparison. These adjustments themselves generated new behaviors.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:08.340
Some users treated the freezes as additional mechanics to game rather than genuine relief

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:14.540
valves. The broader pattern persists. Most major fitness and language apps retain core

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:20.600
gamification layers, though product teams now speak more cautiously about sustainable engagement

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:27.100
and intrinsic motivation. Research continues on whether lighter versions of the same mechanics can

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:33.300
avoid the earlier treadmill effect. Incentive structures reliably steer effort toward whatever is

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:39.440
counted and displayed, often at the expense of unmeasured dimensions such as depth or recovery.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:45.920
When external markers become the primary signal of success, participants tend to protect the marker

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:52.240
even after the original goal has receded. Designers and managers therefore benefit from asking

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:58.920
what activity would look like if the visible score disappeared tomorrow. The same logic now appears

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:05.620
in emerging AI coaching tools and corporate wellness dashboards. The question is whether those systems

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:11.000
will repeat the earlier pattern or incorporate safeguards that keep metrics and service of the

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:17.520
underlying activity, rather than the reverse. We'll leave it there for today. Thanks for thinking

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:22.940
through this one with me. If you know someone who loves a good cautionary tale, send it their way,

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:27.220
and I'll see you tomorrow with another. And if you'd rather watch than listen,

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:32.780
find us on YouTube at Narra Network, links in the show notes, and before you go,

